045417] [17592186153438 #edb/ref 1064 "Two variables, $x$ and $y,$ are said to have a **proportional relationship** if their ratio is constant. That is, for any $(x,y)$ pair, we have\n\n$$\nk = \\dfrac{y}{x}\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is a fixed number called the **constant of proportionality** or **constant of variation.**\n\nAs a concrete example, let's consider the table below. \n\n| $x$ | $y$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ |\n| $2$ | $4$ |\n| $4$ | $8$ |\n\nDo $x$ and $y$ have a proportional relationship?\n\nTo determine whether $x$ and $y$ have a proportional relationship, we first add a new $\\dfrac yx$ column:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $\\dfrac{y}{x}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ |  |\n| $2$ | $4$ |  |\n| $4$ | $8$ |  |\n\nNext, we compute $\\dfrac yx$ for each row:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $\\dfrac{y}{x}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $2\\div 1 = {\\color{blue}{2}}$ |\n| $2$ | $4$ | $4\\div 2 = {\\color{blue}{2}}$ |\n| $4$ | $8$ | $8\\div 4 = {\\color{blue}{2}}$ |\n\nSince all the entries in the third column have the same value, this table does indeed represent a proportional relationship. In this case, the constant of proportionality $k = {\\color{blue}{2}}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153438 #edb/ref 1065 8207 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153438 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "935405f7-2fd3-5fb6-a9ef-e3d74aa721b6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153438 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153439 #edb/ref 1064 "When the variables $x$ and $y$ form a proportional relationship, the ratio of any $(x,y)$ pair is constant:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac y x = k\n$$\n\nUsing the relationship between multiplication and division, we can rewrite this equation above as follows:\n\n$$\ny = kx\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153439 #edb/ref 1065 8208 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153439 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "d635aed4-07fa-5ffc-a25c-f9d760dbed7f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153439 #edb/ref 1067 "Finding the Equation of a Proportional Relationship" #edb/r